1
00:00:02,650 --> 00:00:06,817
Every morning, a mirror shows you who you are.

2
00:00:07,050 --> 00:00:12,244
One Tuesday, Theo’s mirror showed him who he was going to be.

3
00:00:13,050 --> 00:00:15,111
THEO: Wait. What?

4
00:00:17,250 --> 00:00:19,344
THEO: Hello? Hey!

5
00:00:21,250 --> 00:00:26,931
The man in the glass was ten years older. And he wasn’t copying him.

6
00:00:38,850 --> 00:00:42,650
He did what anyone would do. He tested it.

7
00:00:42,650 --> 00:00:44,385
THEO: Left hand.

8
00:00:45,650 --> 00:00:49,621
The reflection followed. A moment too late.

9
00:00:50,050 --> 00:00:53,450
Every face in a mirror is a little out of date.

10
00:00:53,450 --> 00:01:00,473
Stand a metre from the glass, and the light takes about seven billionths of a second to come back to you.

11
00:01:02,450 --> 00:01:06,950
Theo’s mirror was ten years out. In the wrong direction.

12
00:01:06,950 --> 00:01:12,550
Same nose. Less hair. Tired eyes. An office badge.

13
00:01:12,550 --> 00:01:19,454
His cello, shut and dusty. And on the wall, an audition poster. Crossed out.

14
00:01:20,650 --> 00:01:23,175
THEO: Okay. Phone camera.

15
00:01:24,050 --> 00:01:26,679
THEO: Normal. Totally normal.

16
00:01:28,650 --> 00:01:33,754
The hall mirror was normal too. Only the bathroom one knew.

17
00:01:40,450 --> 00:01:43,831
THEO: Nan, does your mirror ever show you older?

18
00:01:44,650 --> 00:01:49,650
NAN: Every morning, love. It’s called being seventy-eight.

19
00:01:49,650 --> 00:01:54,028
THEO: No, I mean ten years older. And he looked like he’d given up.

20
00:01:55,050 --> 00:01:57,402
NAN: Given up on what?

21
00:01:57,650 --> 00:02:00,275
THEO: On me. He gave up on me.

22
00:02:01,250 --> 00:02:06,466
NAN: Then he’s a stranger, love. You haven’t met him yet.

23
00:02:07,250 --> 00:02:13,742
Psychologists asked over nineteen thousand people how much they’d change in the next ten years.

24
00:02:14,050 --> 00:02:19,650
Not much, they said. But people ten years older said they’d changed a lot.

25
00:02:19,650 --> 00:02:25,498
We always think we’re nearly finished. It’s called the end of history illusion.

26
00:02:31,050 --> 00:02:34,498
The next morning, the man was still there.

27
00:02:38,050 --> 00:02:42,786
On the way to work, Theo saw the poster from the mirror.

28
00:02:43,250 --> 00:02:48,450
Port Alder Orchestra. Auditions. In seventy days.

29
00:02:48,450 --> 00:02:50,347
THEO: Seventy days.

30
00:02:55,450 --> 00:03:01,973
He walked to work. The next morning, the man in the glass stood a little straighter.

31
00:03:02,250 --> 00:03:07,104
That night, he opened the cello for the first time in two years.

32
00:03:12,650 --> 00:03:14,999
THEO: Sorry! Sorry.

33
00:03:15,650 --> 00:03:20,387
He was rusty. And seventy days is not very long.

34
00:03:22,050 --> 00:03:25,941
In the glass, the man had stopped looking at him.

35
00:03:26,250 --> 00:03:29,066
THEO: Hey. Hey! Look at me!

36
00:03:32,650 --> 00:03:41,850
Here’s the strange part. When people imagine themselves in ten years, their brains respond a bit like they’re thinking about a stranger.

37
00:03:41,850 --> 00:03:49,579
In one experiment, people who met an aged picture of themselves set aside more than twice as much for retirement.

38
00:03:50,250 --> 00:03:56,759
Theo didn’t have a picture. He had a witness. And the witness was losing hope.

39
00:04:00,650 --> 00:04:06,050
New habits take about sixty-six days, on average, to become automatic.

40
00:04:06,050 --> 00:04:12,473
Some take eighteen. Some take over two hundred. Theo had sixty-four.

41
00:04:22,250 --> 00:04:27,247
Six days before the audition, he took the cello up to the roof.

42
00:04:46,850 --> 00:04:49,832
NAN: Not bad at all, love!

43
00:04:54,050 --> 00:04:56,229
THEO: Nan? Nan!

44
00:05:08,250 --> 00:05:14,336
Three days before the audition, the mirror showed the bathroom. Just the bathroom.

45
00:05:14,450 --> 00:05:15,934
THEO: Hello?

46
00:05:17,050 --> 00:05:18,633
THEO: Where are you?

47
00:05:20,050 --> 00:05:27,673
No man. No office badge. Not even the cello. Ten years from now, nobody lived here.

48
00:05:30,250 --> 00:05:33,588
THEO: He’s gone. The one in the mirror is gone.

49
00:05:34,350 --> 00:05:36,964
NAN: Gone where, love?

50
00:05:37,250 --> 00:05:41,822
THEO: I don’t know. And I’m not going to Port Alder. Not with you like this.

51
00:05:42,650 --> 00:05:47,212
NAN: Don’t you dare stay for me. Did you look properly?

52
00:05:57,050 --> 00:06:00,536
He hadn’t vanished. He’d moved out.

53
00:06:00,650 --> 00:06:02,318
THEO: Port Alder.

54
00:06:04,250 --> 00:06:10,127
Tucked in the frame was a postcard from the sea. In his own handwriting.

55
00:06:10,250 --> 00:06:16,062
The future hadn’t ended. It had moved. But only if he went.

56
00:06:34,250 --> 00:06:38,927
He had one more night. He played until his fingers hurt.

57
00:07:00,850 --> 00:07:05,050
On the last morning, Theo didn’t check the mirror.

58
00:07:05,050 --> 00:07:07,667
He had seen enough to start.

59
00:07:13,050 --> 00:07:17,050
NAN: Go on, love. And send me a postcard.

60
00:07:17,050 --> 00:07:19,123
THEO: I think I already did.

61
00:07:30,650 --> 00:07:35,386
The train left at six. The audition was at nine.

62
00:07:46,250 --> 00:07:48,412
PANEL: Whenever you’re ready.

63
00:07:58,550 --> 00:08:00,701
PANEL: Would you like to stop?

64
00:08:01,850 --> 00:08:04,405
THEO: No. I’m not stopping.

65
00:08:09,550 --> 00:08:13,221
PANEL: Thank you. We’ll be in touch.

66
00:08:15,650 --> 00:08:17,526
And they were.

67
00:08:29,250 --> 00:08:34,850
Ten years later, in a flat by the sea, a man stepped up to a mirror.

68
00:08:34,850 --> 00:08:40,961
It showed him at forty-four. And this time, the man in the glass waved first.

69
00:08:41,650 --> 00:08:43,180
THEO: Hi, you.

70
00:08:45,650 --> 00:08:52,649
He didn’t ask what he’d become. He knew the trick now. It gets decided every morning.
